 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nadditional parcel in township 40",
    "basePrintNo": "S5856",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5856-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §§9-1906 & 9-1908, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5856\n\nSPONSOR: LITTLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to the additional parcel in township 40\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend the Township Forty Settlement Act,\nTitle 19 of Article 9 of the Environmental Conservation Law (\"ECL\"),\nauthorizing resolution of one additional competing claim of title\nbetween the State and a private party in Township Forty, Totten and\nCrossfield Purchase (Raquette Lake), in the Town of Long Lake, Hamilton\nCounty (Township Forty).\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend Title 19 of Article 9 of the ECL,\ntitled Township Forty Settlement Act to add an additional disputed\nparcel and to specify a process for clearing title to the additional\ndisputed parcel. The occupant of the additional contested parcel would\nbe required to make payment to the Town of Long Lake (the Town), which\nwould apply such payments towards the acquisition of land acquired under\nthe Township 40 Settlement Act. The occupant of the additional contested\nparcel would be permitted to convey land in fee to the state or an ease-\nment to the Town, the value of which would reduce the payment due for\nthe contested parcel. The State would relinquish its claim to the\ncontested parcel once the occupant makes the payment to the Town and the\nTown makes the payment to the grantor who conveyed the parcel to the\nState as described in chapter 231 of the Laws of 2016.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for severability.\n\nSection 3 of the bill provides that the act shall take effect on the\nthirtieth day after it becomes law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2013, the voters approved an amendment to Article XIV, Section I of\nthe New York State Constitution, authorizing the State to resolve\ncompeting claims of title between the State and private individuals in\nTownship Forty, Totten and Crossfield purchase in the Town of Long Lake,\nHamilton County, more commonly known as the Hamlet of Raquette Lake.\nLegislation implementing the constitutional amendment was also passed in\n2013 and is found at Title 19 of Article 9 of ECL.\n\nCurrently, ECL § 9-1905 lists 216 specific disputed parcels that would\nbe eligible for participation in the Township 40 settlement and ECL.\n9-1907 set forth a process through which the State would relinquish its\nclaim of title on those parcels. Each of those claiming title to the 216\ndisputed parcels participated in the settlement and made the requisite\npayments to the Town of Long Lake. Certain lands along the Marion River\nwere identified as potentially satisfying the \"net benefit\" requirement\nand the legislature approved the acquisition of those lands in 2016\n(Chapter 231 of the laws of 2016).  The State then acquired title to the\nland and, in the summer of 2017, relinquished its claim to the 216\ndisputed parcels.\n\n\nDue to an earlier court decision, when the implementing legislation was\nenacted into law in 2013, it was believed that the parcel that is the\nsubject of this bill was not disputed. However, a court decision in the\nspring of 2017 vacated the earlier decision, meaning that the parcel is,\nin fact, disputed and should therefore have been included in the list of\ndisputed parcels contained in ECL § 9-1905.\n\nMoreover, equitable considerations require that the instant parcel be\nincluded in the settlement. More than twenty years ago those claiming\ntitle to the additional disputed parcel subdivided the land they\nclaimed. They kept a subdivided parcel for themselves, and sold six\nsubdivided parcels to other parties. Each of these seven parcels has\nexactly the same chain of title. The six parcels sold to third parties\nall were allowed to participate in the settlement, but the parcel\nretained by the claimants of the additional dispute parcel was kept out\nof the settlement. Equity mandates that those claiming title to the\nadditional disputed parcel be treated the same way as those claiming\ntitle to the other six subdivided parcels.\n\nThis proposal will allow those claiming title to the additional disputed\nparcel to participate in the settlement on the same terms and conditions\nas those who claimed title to the 216 parcels that were listed in ECL§\n9-1905. These new claimants will be required to make a payment to the\nTown of Long Lake, using the formula set forth in law, which the Town\nwill then pay to the grantor who conveyed title to the Marion River\nproperty to the People of the State of New York for inclusion in the\nForest Preserve. Such payment would be in recognition that the grantor\ntook a considerable loss in conveying title to the Marion River parcel\nto the State. After such payment is complete, DEC will relinquish its\nclaim to the additional disputed parcel by filing a survey map relin-\nquishing the claim in the Hamilton County Clerk's office.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019 - S.7939 - Passed Senate\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThirtieth day after the act shall become law.\n",
